  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA convective line is expected to pass, mostly north of\nPittsburgh, with the remnants of overnight convection. In the\nlast few hours, chances of severe along this line have \nincreased as storms have ramped up and recouple in a 30-35kt \n0-3km shear. The primary threats with this will be damaging\nwinds, up to 60-70mph, with a low-end tornado threat, more\nlikely is storms take a dive southeast. In the last hour, this\nconvection has developed upscale on the southeast side for the\npassing MCV, which has prompted the issuance of a severe \nthunderstorm watch across most of the area (save parts of \nnorthern West Virginia.\n\nAfter a brief break later this morning, additional convection \nmay arrive by midday/early afternoon, likely the continuation of\nactivity currently near the southern end of Lake Michigan. \nAlthough, early convection may complicate these chances. Another\nround is then possible after sunset with a shortwave passage. \nWhile exact timing and evolution of afternoon/evening convection\nremains in question, there is still a decent level of \nconfidence in the presence of an environment conducive for \nsevere storms. Model soundings suggest mean-layer CAPE values \nwill be in the 1500 to 2000 J/kg range during the peak heating \nperiod, with deep-layer shear on the order of 25 to 35 knots. \nThis will support organized convection with mainly a damaging \nwind threat, both from cold pool consolidation and downburst \npotential (DCAPE of around 1000 J/kg). Some at least transient \nsupercell structures/rotating storms are also possible, thus the\nthreat for a tornado or two cannot be ignored.\n\nFor Tuesday (and potentially Wednesday as well), the main threat\ncorridor shifts south a bit, with the best potential for severe\nstorms mainly south of I-70 (matching SPC's day 2 Slight Risk \narea). This is where richer instability will reside during the \nafternoon and evening. Again, damaging winds appear to be the \nprimary threat, as this may be the more favored region for \nconvective complex passage. The more concentrated wind damage \nthreat area likely remains just to the south of our region, but \ncould clip portions of at least eastern Ohio.\n\nIn addition to severe threats, there remains the risk of flash\nflooding. Given precipitable water values lifting back into the\n1.6 to 1.7 inch range (90th to 95th percentile in the \nclimatology), as well as the potential for multiple thunderstorm\ncomplexes, WPC continues to highlight our region in a Slight \n(level 2 of 4) Risk for excessive rain for today into Tuesday \nnight, with the main heavy rain threat area shifting south of \nthe Mason-Dixon Line by Wednesday. 48-hour NBM mean \nprecipitation totals are in the 1.5 to 2.5 inch range generally \nsouth of US-422, with 90th percentile totals in the same area of\n3 to 4 inches. Given the still relatively moist soil across the\nregion, such rainfall totals could create at least isolated \nflash flooding issues in the heavier convection. Still, with the\nuncertainty in timing and location of the greatest potential, \nwill hold off on the issuance of a Flood Watch at this time.\n\nBy Thursday, both heavy rain and severe weather threats should \nbegin to subside as a stronger Great Lakes shortwave pushes a\nboundary across the area, leading to less threatening weather.",
